<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hadewijch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 13th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hadewijch was a Brabantine mystic and poet of the thirteenth century, probably a beguine living in or near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. Her visions, letters and poems in Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> describe the soul's love for God in the language of courtly love, minne. Rediscovered in the nineteenth century, her work is counted among the masterpieces of medieval Dutch literature and of Christian mysticism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hadewijch.jpg" alt="Hadewijch" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hadewijch (afschrift) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadewijch_gedicht1_HsGent_f49r.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
